  "body_mappings": [
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        518.4614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "A moth skims over an ultraviolet lamp and flips upside down, belly to the sky. In Costa Rica's misty Monteverde cloud-forest, stereo cameras caught such acrobatics for a 2024 Imperial College study that unravelled an age-old puzzle, the helpless swirl of insects round porch lights."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        430.7614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.9614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Folk wisdom blames the circling on attraction. In fact, insects instinctively keep their backs to whatever shines brightest, normally the sky, to stay level. A lamp, a counterfeit sky, hijacks this built-in habit, leaving insects eddying in its halo or inverted above it. In 477 field clips, precious few darted straight at the light."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        323.2614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        422.2614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Many fireflies flirt with their lanterns. A male blinks an overture, a female winks back, and bright nights smother the banter. At Tufts University, suitors under artificial light mustered about five flash patterns a minute instead of ten. Females reciprocated about half as often under red or blue light, scarcely at all under white, and never under amber."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        215.7614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        314.7614173228347
      ],
      "text": "The harm spills into the ecosystem. Along 55 Thames Valley roadsides, lamp-lit hedges held 47% fewer moth caterpillars than dark ones, with harsh white LEDs worse than mellower sodium lamps. Street lamps rigged up in seven of 14 Swiss meadows cut nocturnal pollinator visits by 62%. The plants set fewer fruit, and daytime pollinators proved poor understudies."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        128.0614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        207.2614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Over 80% of humanity lives beneath a luminous veil, which hides the pale ribbon of the Milky Way from more than a third. Citizen scientists' star counts from 2011 to 2022 show the sky brightening about 9.6% a year. At that pace, a child born where 250 stars twinkle would see about 100 by their eighteenth birthday."
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        517.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "一只飞蛾掠过一盏紫外灯，随即上下颠倒地翻过身来，肚皮朝天。在哥斯达黎加蒙特韦尔德雾气缭绕的云雾林里，立体摄像机为伦敦帝国理工学院2024年的一项研究拍下了这类杂技动作。这项研究解开了一个由来已久的谜团，那就是昆虫为什么会身不由己地绕着门廊灯打转。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        429.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "民间的老说法把这种打转归咎于吸引。事实上，昆虫会本能地让背部朝向最亮的东西，那通常是天空，以此保持平稳。灯成了一片冒牌的天空，劫持了这种与生俱来的习性，让昆虫在它的光晕里打着旋，或在它上方翻转过来。在477段野外视频片段中，径直冲向灯光的昆虫寥寥无几。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        340.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        420.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "许多萤火虫用自己的灯笼来调情。雄虫闪烁着奏出一段序曲，雌虫眨眼般回应，而太亮的夜晚会淹没这番调笑。在塔夫茨大学，处在人造光下的求偶者每分钟只勉强打出约五组闪光，而不是十组。雌虫在红光或蓝光下回应的次数约减半，在白光下几乎不回应，在琥珀色光下则从不回应。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        252.16141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        332.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "危害还会蔓延到整个生态系统。在泰晤士河谷的55处路边，有灯照明的树篱里，蛾类毛虫比没灯的地方少47%，而刺眼的白色LED比更柔和的钠灯更糟。在瑞士14片草甸中的7片，临时架起的路灯让夜间传粉者的造访减少了62%。植物结的果实变少了，而白天的传粉者也只是不称职的替补。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        163.66141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        243.66141732283472
      ],
      "text": "全球80%以上的人类生活在一层发光的帷幕之下，它让三分之一以上的人看不见银河那条淡淡的光带。公民科学家2011年至2022年的数星记录显示，夜空每年约变亮9.6%。按这个速度，若一个孩子出生在能看到250颗星星闪耀的地方，到18岁生日时只能看到约100颗。"
    }
  ],
